1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
You've woken up with Yuta Okkotsu's Copy, from

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,200
Jujutsu Kaisen.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,640
In real life.

4
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,700
It can copy anything you watch someone do.

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,380
And the thing standing behind you decides what

6
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:11,820
it costs.

7
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,060
In forty days, five of the things you've

8
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,760
copied will save fourteen people in a flooded

9
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,100
tunnel.

10
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:21,240
But right now it's eleven forty-seven at night,

11
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:23,900
there's a ring on your finger you've never

12
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,420
seen, and something huge is breathing behind you.

13
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:30,880
In the story, the ring was a promise.

14
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,860
Rika Orimoto was Yuta's friend when they were

15
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:34,220
children.

16
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,480
She gave him her mother's ring and promised

17
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:37,120
to marry him.

18
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,480
Then she died young.

19
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:40,720
Yuta couldn't let go.

20
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:44,200
Her soul stayed, tied to his cursed energy,

21
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:46,400
and it became a special-grade curse,

22
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:48,180
the Queen of Curses.

23
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:49,940
Now it's behind you.

24
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:50,980
Pale,

25
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,580
huge, all teeth, and standing very close.

26
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:56,840
Your mum walks past the door and says

27
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:57,840
goodnight to the wardrobe.

28
00:00:58,580 --> 00:00:59,260
It doesn't attack.

29
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:01,080
It just looks at the ring.

30
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:04,440
Lesson one: it isn't a weapon.

31
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,560
It's a promise with a very big shadow.

32
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:11,760
In the story, Copy lets Yuta reproduce other

33
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:12,620
people's techniques.

34
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:15,880
And nobody is completely sure of the exact

35
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:16,440
conditions.

36
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:19,380
So, at breakfast, you watch your dad flip

37
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,280
an omelette.

38
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:21,860
You've never flipped one in your life.

39
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:23,760
Your hands do it.

40
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:25,240
Perfectly.

41
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:27,640
The little tilt of the wrist, the fold

42
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,780
at the end, even the way he blows

43
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:31,380
a strand of hair out of his eyes.

44
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:33,920
You try the second one on your own,

45
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:35,580
and it's a disaster.

46
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,540
Lesson two: you copy what you see,

47
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,320
not what you understand.

48
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:43,380
At lunch you stop in front of a

49
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:44,420
busker with a guitar.

50
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,120
You watch his hands for one song.

51
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,620
Then you borrow the guitar and play his

52
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:53,680
whole piece, note for note, the little slides

53
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:54,000
and all.

54
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,060
A small crowd stops.

55
00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:57,780
Somebody films it.

56
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,460
He stares at you like you've stolen something,

57
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:02,900
and he isn't entirely wrong.

58
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:05,320
Then your fingers start to shake.

59
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,980
They can do it, but they've never done

60
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,820
it before, and the body notices.

61
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:14,000
Lesson three: copying a skill isn't the same

62
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,580
as being built for it.

63
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:18,660
In the evening you watch a climber on

64
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:20,820
a wall, then copy every move.

65
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:23,840
Halfway up, you realise you copied his reach

66
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,480
but not his arms.

67
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:26,840
You slip.

68
00:02:27,740 --> 00:02:30,060
Something huge and pale grabs you by the

69
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:32,720
hood, in mid-air, and sets you down very

70
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:33,120
gently.

71
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:34,820
It looks at you.

72
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:39,805
It doesn't look pleased.

73
00:02:39,805 --> 00:02:39,845
You never find out if that was a

74
00:02:39,845 --> 00:02:40,780
rescue or a warning.

75
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:41,940
Lesson four:

76
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:43,580
she's not your servant.

77
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,260
Sometimes she catches you anyway.

78
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:49,560
On day three, you have to give a

79
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:50,780
presentation at work.

80
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:54,260
In the film, Yuta uses Cursed Speech, the

81
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:56,600
technique that belongs to Toge Inumaki's family.

82
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:59,440
It's normally passed down only inside that clan.

83
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:00,940
You don't have that.

84
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:03,760
But you've copied your old teacher's voice, the

85
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:09,800
one that made a whole classroom go quiet.

86
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:09,827
You open your mouth, and the room stops.

87
00:03:09,827 --> 00:03:10,780
It's a little too good.

88
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:13,820
A colleague goes pale and asks you not

89
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:14,680
to do that again.

90
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,300
Lesson five: when you copy a voice, you

91
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,020
copy what it does to other people.

92
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:23,820
On day six, your friend says something odd.

93
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,900
You've started talking like the man who shouted

94
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:27,840
at you on the bus.

95
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:29,960
You didn't decide it.

96
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:31,800
You just watched him, and it stuck.

97
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:36,020
In nineteen sixty-one, the psychologist Albert Bandura showed

98
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,160
that children who watched an adult hit an

99
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:41,680
inflatable doll later did the same, without being

100
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:42,220
told to.

101
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,920
We copy more than we think.

102
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:47,600
You count how many strangers you've copied this

103
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:47,840
week.

104
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:50,020
You stop at twenty.

105
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,900
Lesson six: you don't choose everything you copy.

106
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,780
A boy films you in the park: first

107
00:03:56,780 --> 00:04:00,700
juggling, then a handstand, then a perfect cartwheel.

108
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:02,120
He posts it.

109
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:03,740
People say it's editing.

110
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:05,940
People say you're hiding something.

111
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:09,560
Nobody says "Rika", because nobody knows who that

112
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:12,240
is, and the few who do think it's

113
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:12,560
a joke.

114
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,440
Lesson seven: the ones who'd understand are the

115
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,060
ones who'd never believe it.

116
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,600
On day twelve you watch a dancer rehearse

117
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,980
in a studio, through the window, for twenty

118
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:24,320
minutes.

119
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,760
Then you walk in and do the whole

120
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:29,860
routine, every turn, every pause, as if you'd

121
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:31,020
practised it for years.

122
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,020
Her teacher claps.

123
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:35,140
She wants you in the show on Friday.

124
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:39,020
On Friday, the lights come on, and you

125
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:39,280
freeze.

126
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:41,620
Your feet know every step.

127
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:43,860
Your stomach doesn't.

128
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:47,140
Someone huge and pale stands in the wings,

129
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,040
and you could swear she's covering her face.

130
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:54,240
Lesson eight: you can copy the steps, but

131
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:55,920
not the nerves that go with them.

132
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:59,180
On day fifteen, an old chess player lets

133
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:00,480
you sit down across from him.

134
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,360
You copy his opening, move for move.

135
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:04,560
It's beautiful.

136
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:07,460
Then he makes a move you've never seen,

137
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,100
and you don't know why.

138
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:10,940
You lose in eleven turns.

139
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:12,180
He smiles.

140
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:14,680
"You have my hands," he says.

141
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:16,620
"You don't have my reasons."

142
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:20,000
Lesson nine: Copy shows you the move.

143
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,060
It never shows you the why.

144
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:25,620
On day seventeen you notice something.

145
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:28,660
Every skill you copy stays in your hands,

146
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:30,080
but not all at once.

147
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:32,140
You can hold five of them at a

148
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:32,420
time.

149
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:34,900
The sixth pushes the oldest one out, like

150
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:36,100
a drawer that's too full.

151
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:38,140
You write the five on a sheet of

152
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:40,240
paper, and stick it on the wardrobe, because

153
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:40,940
you keep forgetting.

154
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:43,560
It looks like a very strange shopping list.

155
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:48,060
Your mum asks what "locksmith, swimmer, climber, driver,

156
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:49,720
strongman" is for.

157
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,120
You say: "Just in case."

158
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:55,700
Lesson ten: choose carefully what you keep.

159
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:57,280
You can't keep everything.

160
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:01,080
On day twenty you take the ring off,

161
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:02,040
to see what happens.

162
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:05,780
In the story, Yuta calls Rika out using

163
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:06,460
that ring.

164
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:09,420
And her cursed energy is vast.

165
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:11,300
The lights go out.

166
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,740
The room gets very cold.

167
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,840
Something huge unfolds behind you, taller than the

168
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:21,060
ceiling, and the walls start to hum.

169
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,100
You put the ring back on.

170
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:25,460
It all folds away, slowly.

171
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:28,800
She never moved from where she was.

172
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:31,840
Lesson eleven: the ring isn't a leash.

173
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:33,440
It's a door,

174
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:36,160
and you're the only one who can close

175
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:36,620
it.

176
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:40,180
On day twenty-five, a man collapses on a

177
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:41,620
pavement in front of you.

178
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:43,020
People step back.

179
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:45,200
Someone is already shouting for an ambulance.

180
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:48,200
Months ago, on a training video, you watched

181
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,020
a paramedic do chest compressions.

182
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:51,540
You've never done one.

183
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,860
You kneel down.

184
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:54,740
Your hands do it.

185
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:55,420
Steady,

186
00:06:56,260 --> 00:06:56,460
strong,

187
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:58,920
in rhythm, and you keep going while your

188
00:06:58,920 --> 00:06:59,700
arms burn.

189
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:01,820
You don't know why it works.

190
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:04,360
You only know it works until the real

191
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:05,440
paramedics arrive.

192
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,600
They take over, look at you, and ask

193
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:09,200
where you trained.

194
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,620
Lesson twelve: sometimes you don't need to understand

195
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:13,340
the move.

196
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:15,580
You only need to buy someone time.

197
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:19,380
By day thirty you have a hundred skills

198
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:21,300
in your hands, and not one you can

199
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:21,640
explain.

200
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:24,600
You're tired in a way sleep doesn't fix.

201
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:27,600
You can do anything you've watched, and you've

202
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,320
forgotten how to do the things you used

203
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:29,740
to love.

204
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:31,480
You find your old guitar.

205
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:33,900
You can't play it any more.

206
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:35,140
Only the busker's song.

207
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:39,400
Lesson thirteen: you can borrow a lot, but

208
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,580
you can't borrow a self.

209
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:45,000
On day forty, the rain is so heavy

210
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,680
that an underpass floods around a stopped bus,

211
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:49,580
with fourteen people inside.

212
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:51,900
You're the only one who can do anything.

213
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:54,680
You have a hundred skills in your hands,

214
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:56,460
but only five fit.

215
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:58,580
You swap them in your head, the way

216
00:07:58,580 --> 00:07:59,320
you'd swap cards:

217
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,460
the old chess player goes, the dancer goes,

218
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:03,920
the busker goes.

219
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:10,356
The swimmer's stroke, to get across.

220
00:08:10,356 --> 00:08:10,546
The climber's grip, to reach the roof.

221
00:08:10,546 --> 00:08:12,200
The driver's calm, to open the door.

222
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:15,120
The locksmith's fingers, for the jammed window.

223
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:17,840
The strongman's back, to hold it.

224
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:19,940
You put the ring on your finger and

225
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:20,480
say nothing.

226
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:22,640
There isn't time to say anything.

227
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:25,920
Something huge and pale rises behind you,

228
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,000
and the water goes very still.

229
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:30,540
For the first time, it doesn't look at

230
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:30,880
the ring.

231
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:32,460
It looks at the bus.

232
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,900
You get them out one by one.

233
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:38,780
Fourteen people on a wet pavement, shaking, wrapped

234
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:40,300
in whatever coats were nearby.

235
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:42,820
Nobody asks what you are.

236
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,240
The next morning you can't lift your arms,

237
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:48,040
and she's sitting at the foot of your

238
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:48,300
bed.

239
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:50,460
In the story, after the film,

240
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,740
Rika's soul moves on, and Yuta keeps using

241
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:54,220
her power.

242
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:56,540
Yours doesn't move on.

243
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:57,660
She stays.

244
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,860
You say thank you, out loud, to a

245
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:03,480
wardrobe, and for a moment the ring feels

246
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:03,860
warm.

247
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:08,100
Lesson fourteen: the strongest thing you copied wasn't

248
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:08,520
a skill.

249
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:11,320
It was a promise to keep going.

250
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:15,480
The next night, at eleven forty-seven, you look

251
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:16,420
at the ring on your finger.

252
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:19,920
So if you woke up tomorrow with Yuta's

253
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:22,920
Copy, what's the first thing you'd copy?

254
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:25,460
And would you take off the ring, to

255
00:09:25,460 --> 00:09:26,460
see what's behind it?

256
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:27,480
Tell me in the comments.
